  "lean_proofs": "/-\n# The Discrete Hodge Laplacian, its Harmonic Space, and Diffusion Message Passing\n\nA self-contained foundation for the discrete Hodge program over a two-step\ncochain complex of finite-dimensional real inner-product spaces\n\n    U --e--> V --d--> W ,\n\nstudying the Hodge Laplacian on the middle space V,\n\n    Δ = d* ∘ d + e ∘ e* ,\n\nwith d* = adjoint d and e* = adjoint e.  The organizing identity is the\nsum-of-squares Dirichlet energy\n\n    ⟪Δ x, x⟫ = ‖d x‖² + ‖e* x‖² .\n-/\n\nnamespace HodgeLaplacianGreen\n\nvariable {U V W : Type*}\n  [NormedAddCommGroup U] [InnerProductSpace ℝ U] [FiniteDimensional ℝ U]\n  [NormedAddCommGroup V] [InnerProductSpace ℝ V] [FiniteDimensional ℝ V]\n  [NormedAddCommGroup W] [InnerProductSpace ℝ W] [FiniteDimensional ℝ W]\n\n/-- Hodge Laplacian Δ = d* ∘ d + e ∘ e* on the middle space V. -/\nnoncomputable def hodgeLap (e : U →ₗ[ℝ] V) (d : V →ₗ[ℝ] W) : V →ₗ[ℝ] V :=\n  LinearMap.adjoint d ∘ₗ d + e ∘ₗ LinearMap.adjoint e\n\n-- Δ x = d* (d x) + e (e* x)\ntheorem hodgeLap_apply (x : V) :\n    hodgeLap e d x = LinearMap.adjoint d (d x) + e (LinearMap.adjoint e x)\n\n-- Δ is self-adjoint:  ⟪Δ x, y⟫ = ⟪x, Δ y⟫\ntheorem hodgeLap_isSymmetric : (hodgeLap e d).IsSymmetric\n\n-- Dirichlet identity:  ⟪Δ x, x⟫ = ‖d x‖² + ‖e* x‖²\ntheorem hodgeLap_quadratic_form (x : V) :\n    (⟪hodgeLap e d x, x⟫_ℝ) = ‖d x‖ ^ 2 + ‖LinearMap.adjoint e x‖ ^ 2\n\n-- harmonic ⇔ closed & co-closed:  Δ x = 0 ↔ d x = 0 ∧ e* x = 0\ntheorem hodgeLap_apply_eq_zero_iff (x : V) :\n    hodgeLap e d x = 0 ↔ d x = 0 ∧ LinearMap.adjoint e x = 0\n\n-- strict positivity off the kernel:  ⟪Δ x, x⟫ = 0 ↔ Δ x = 0\ntheorem hodgeLap_quadratic_eq_zero_iff (x : V) :\n    (⟪hodgeLap e d x, x⟫_ℝ) = 0 ↔ hodgeLap e d x = 0\n\n-- image of Δ is orthogonal to the kernel:  Δ x ∈ (ker Δ)ᗮ\ntheorem hodgeLap_apply_mem_orthogonal_ker (x : V) :\n    hodgeLap e d x ∈ (LinearMap.ker (hodgeLap e d))ᗮ\n\n/-- One explicit-Euler diffusion step S = id − a·Δ. -/\nnoncomputable def diffStep (a : ℝ) : V →ₗ[ℝ] V :=\n  LinearMap.id - a • hodgeLap e d\n\n/-- Harmonic projection: orthogonal projection onto ker Δ. -/\nnoncomputable def harmonicProjection : V →L[ℝ] (LinearMap.ker (hodgeLap e d)) :=\n  (LinearMap.ker (hodgeLap e d)).orthogonalProjection\n\n-- harmonic cochains are fixed by one diffusion step\ntheorem diffStep_harmonic_fixed (a : ℝ) {h : V} (hh : hodgeLap e d h = 0) :\n    diffStep e d a h = h\n\n-- harmonic cochains are fixed at every depth:  Sᵏ h = h\ntheorem diffStep_pow_harmonic_fixed (a : ℝ) {h : V} (hh : hodgeLap e d h = 0) (k : ℕ) :\n    ((diffStep e d a) ^ k) h = h\n\n-- harmonic projection conserved in one step:  P (S x) = P x\ntheorem harmonicProjection_diffStep (a : ℝ) (x : V) :\n    harmonicProjection e d (diffStep e d a x) = harmonicProjection e d x\n\n-- harmonic projection conserved at every depth:  P (Sᵏ x) = P x\ntheorem harmonicProjection_diffStep_pow (a : ℝ) (x : V) (k : ℕ) :\n    harmonicProjection e d (((diffStep e d a) ^ k) x) = harmonicProjection e d x\n\nend HodgeLaplacianGreen\n",
  "future_directions": "# Future Directions — Discrete Hodge Laplacian, Harmonic Space & Diffusion Message Passing\n\n## Synthesis\n\nThis cycle rebuilds the discrete Hodge program on a self-contained foundation for\na two-step cochain complex of finite-dimensional real inner-product spaces\n\n    U --e--> V --d--> W ,        Δ = d* ∘ d + e ∘ e*   on   V ,\n\nwith d* = adjoint d and e* = adjoint e. The single organizing identity is the\nsum-of-squares Dirichlet energy\n\n    ⟪Δ x, x⟫ = ‖d x‖² + ‖e* x‖² ,\n\nfrom which everything else follows. The harmonic space ker Δ is exactly the\nclosed-and-co-closed cochains (d x = 0 ∧ e* x = 0); the Rayleigh form is strictly\npositive off ker Δ; self-adjointness places the image of Δ inside (ker Δ)ᗮ. On\nthe dynamical side, the explicit-Euler diffusion step S = id − a·Δ fixes the\nharmonic space pointwise and conserves the harmonic projection along the whole\ntrajectory, P (Sᵏ x) = P x: diffusion never creates or destroys the topological\n(harmonic) component, it only relaxes the exact / co-exact part. This is the\nlocal-to-global picture made discrete: local data (the maps d, e on individual\ncochains) glues, via the Hodge decomposition, into the global obstruction object\nker Δ.\n\n## Results summary (proved sorry-free)\n\n| Theorem | Statement |\n|---|---|\n| hodgeLap_isSymmetric | Δ is self-adjoint |\n| hodgeLap_quadratic_form | ⟪Δ x, x⟫ = ‖d x‖² + ‖e* x‖² |\n| hodgeLap_apply_eq_zero_iff | Δ x = 0 ↔ d x = 0 ∧ e* x = 0 (harmonic ⇔ closed & co-closed) |\n| hodgeLap_quadratic_eq_zero_iff | ⟪Δ x, x⟫ = 0 ↔ Δ x = 0 (strict positivity off the kernel) |\n| hodgeLap_apply_mem_orthogonal_ker | Δ x ∈ (ker Δ)ᗮ |\n| diffStep_harmonic_fixed / diffStep_pow_harmonic_fixed | Sᵏ h = h for harmonic h |\n| harmonicProjection_diffStep / harmonicProjection_diffStep_pow | P (Sᵏ x) = P x |\n\n## Research directions\n\n### 1. From \"image in (ker Δ)ᗮ\" to the orthogonal splitting range Δ = (ker Δ)ᗮ\nThis cycle proves the easy inclusion Δ x ∈ (ker Δ)ᗮ. Conjecture the full identity\nrange Δ = (ker Δ)ᗮ, and consequently that Δ is a linear isomorphism of (ker Δ)ᗮ\nonto itself. Falsifiable by any y ∈ (ker Δ)ᗮ not of the form Δ x, or any nonzero\nz ∈ (ker Δ)ᗮ with Δ z = 0. Because Δ is self-adjoint and positive semidefinite,\nthis follows from finite-dimensional rank–nullity and the spectral theorem on the\ninvariant complement.\n\n### 2. Bundle the Green's operator into an honest self-adjoint linear map\nConjecture the pointwise solver of Δ z = x − P x on (ker Δ)ᗮ bundles into a\ngenuine G : V →ₗ[ℝ] V with G ∘ Δ = Δ ∘ G = id − P_harmonic, G ∘ P_harmonic = 0,\nand G self-adjoint (⟪G x, y⟫ = ⟪x, G y⟫). The key insight is that uniqueness is\nthe only obstruction to linearity of a solver: z(x+y) and z(x)+z(y) both solve\nthe same ∃! problem (injectivity on the complement forces them equal), so G is\nautomatically additive and homogeneous, with self-adjointness following from\nhodgeLap_isSymmetric restricted to the invariant complement. Falsifiable by any\ncandidate G with Δ (G x) ≠ x − P x, or with ⟪G x, y⟫ ≠ ⟪x, G y⟫.\n\n### 3. The diffusion energy is a strict Lyapunov function with harmonic limit\nConjecture the Dirichlet energy E(x) = ⟪Δ x, x⟫ is non-increasing along\nadmissible diffusion, E(S x) ≤ E(x) for 0 < a < 2/λ_max, with equality iff x is\nharmonic, and that Sᵏ x → P x as k → ∞. The key insight is that\nhodgeLap_quadratic_form already exhibits E as a sum of squares whose zero set is\nexactly ker Δ, and harmonicProjection_diffStep_pow pins the limit's harmonic part\nto P x, so only the complementary part must be shown to vanish. Monotonicity\nreduces to a single algebraic estimate on\nE(x) − E(Sx) = 2a⟪Δx,Δx⟫ − a²⟪Δx,Δ(Δx)⟫ once Δ is bounded by λ_max on the\ncomplement. Falsifiable by an x and admissible a with E(S x) > E(x), or a\nnon-harmonic fixed point of S.\n\n### 4. Quantitative contraction at the spectral-gap rate\nConjecture that for an admissible step 0 < a < 2/λ_max,\n‖Sᵏ x − P x‖ ≤ ρᵏ ‖x − P x‖ with ρ = max_{λ>0} |1 − aλ| < 1 over the nonzero\neigenvalues of Δ. The key insight is that harmonicProjection_diffStep_pow makes\nx − P x the only part that moves, and on (ker Δ)ᗮ the Laplacian has strictly\npositive eigenvalues, so ‖S y‖ ≤ ρ‖y‖ for y ∈ (ker Δ)ᗮ collapses to the\none-dimensional estimate |1 − aλ| ≤ ρ per eigenvector. Falsifiable by a complex,\nan admissible a, and an iterate failing to contract by ρ.\n\n### 5. Spectral resolution Δ = Σ λᵢ Pᵢ with P₀ = P_harmonic and G = Σ_{λᵢ>0} λᵢ⁻¹ Pᵢ\nConjecture the finite-dimensional spectral theorem for Δ: an orthonormal\neigenbasis with 0 = λ₀ ≤ λ₁ ≤ …, the 0-eigenprojection equal to P_harmonic,\nΔ = Σ λᵢ Pᵢ, and the Green's operator equal to G = Σ_{λᵢ>0} λᵢ⁻¹ Pᵢ. The key\ninsight is that hodgeLap_isSymmetric feeds the finite-dimensional spectral theorem\ndirectly, the quadratic form pins the spectrum to [0,∞), and\nhodgeLap_quadratic_eq_zero_iff identifies the 0-eigenspace with ker Δ, so the\neigendecomposition is an application rather than a new theory. Falsifiable by a\nnegative eigenvalue, a non-harmonic 0-eigenvector, or a mismatch G ≠ Σ λᵢ⁻¹ Pᵢ.\n\n### 6. The discrete Hodge isomorphism H = ker d / range e ≃ ker Δ as a quotient isometry\nConjecture that the harmonic representative realizes the cohomology quotient:\nevery class [x] with d x = 0 has a unique harmonic representative P x ∈ ker Δ,\ngiving a linear isomorphism ker d / range e ≃ ker Δ, and that it is an isometry\nfor the quotient norm, ‖[x]‖ = ‖P x‖. The key insight is that range Δ = (ker Δ)ᗮ\nsplits any closed cochain into a harmonic part P x and a part in range Δ;\nrestricted to closed cochains the d*-component vanishes, so x − P x ∈ range e and\nP x is the canonical class representative, with the energy minimization\n‖P x‖ ≤ ‖x − e u‖ making the quotient infimum attained exactly at P x.\nFalsifiable by a closed x whose harmonic projection leaves the class, or a class\nwhose quotient norm differs from ‖P x‖.\n",
